Chapter: ChanLostWakeup

 A producer and a consumer, each its own process, exchange two values
 through a capacity-one blocking channel. The producer fills the channel on
 its first send and blocks on the second; the consumer drains the first
 value, and the rendezvous in the receiver's wake path hands the second
 value across and wakes the producer. Both finish, and proc 0 -- which
 waits on both -- prints "done".

 This is the functional regression test for the channel-block lost-wakeup
 (BACKLOG 4.11g). On a correct compiler it prints "done" cooperatively:
 nothing runs between the producer's capacity check and its commit to
 BLOCKED, so the wake is never lost. The bug lives in a ~15-instruction
 window that no timer reliably hits, so this test cannot trigger it on its
 own -- it is driven to a deterministic hang by a throwaway compiler built
 with the send-block yield mutant. The technique and the mutant diff are in
 docs/Test/Active/YieldInjectionMutant.md.

Section: Body

  producer : Integer -> [Concurrent] Integer
  producer (ch) = act
    a <- (chan-kern-send-block ch 10)
    b <- (chan-kern-send-block ch 20)
    b
  end

  consumer : Integer -> [Concurrent] Integer
  consumer (ch) = act
    x <- (chan-kern-recv-block ch)
    y <- (chan-kern-recv-block ch)
    y
  end

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    ch <- chan-kern-create 1
    p <- process-spawn (\z -> producer ch)
    c <- process-spawn (\z -> consumer ch)
    wp <- process-wait p
    wc <- process-wait c
    print-line-uni "done"
  end
